Phase 0
I would organize the program using the MVC pattern.
The Model will store the current amount of coffee and the pot's maximum capacity.
The View will display the menu and messages, while the Controller will handle commands like brewing, pouring, checking the coffee level, and exiting. The Controller will also make sure the user cannot brew too much coffee or pour more than the pot contains.
The most confusing part of this challenge is understanding how the Model, View, and Controller communicate with each other. I'm also a little unsure about handling invalid commands, but I plan to let the Controller handle those checks.


Phase 1
In this phase, I created the CoffeePotModel class to track the coffee amount and maximum capacity.
I also added checks to prevent negative amounts, overfilling, and pouring more coffee than the pot contains. 
I learned that the Model manages the program's data and protects it from invalid changes, which keeps the program working correctly.

Phase 2
In this phase, I created the CoffeePotView class to display the menu, collect user input, and print messages. 
Keeping these tasks separate makes the code easier to organize and maintain. 
It also keeps the View focused on interacting with the user instead of handling the coffee pot's logic. 




